  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nAfter a bit of morning fog, clear skies returned to most of the\nlocal area this afternoon with a few high clouds filtering in\nfrom the west. With a very weak surface pressure gradient in\nplace, light winds have prevailed, with most automated stations\nindicating winds around 5 MPH or less for most of the day. \nThese light winds, combined with well above normal temperatures,\nhave made for a spectacular Fall day.\n\nFor tonight, expect the continuation of light winds and elevated\ndewpoints to allow for the development of some fog during the\novernight hours, although with increasing high clouds, \nconfidence in widespread and dense fog is lowered. That said, \nboth the 12Z and 18Z HRRR hinted at the potential for some dense\nfog across mainly our western areas towards daybreak Thursday, \nnot unlike what we saw this morning. Given the conflicting \nsignals, settled on just introducing the wording of fog into the\nforecast package for Thursday morning along with some patchy \ndense fog wording to the HWO.\n\nOverall, Thursday is expected to be another nice day across the\nlocal area despite increasing southerly winds of 10 to 20 MPH by\nafternoon. These increasing southerly winds will be in response\nto an increasing pressure gradient as the upper level low off\nthe west coast moves into the Great Basin, and surface low \npressure starts deepening on the lee side of the Rockies. This\nsurface pressure gradient will increase further on Friday,\nmaking for windy day across the local area. While temperatures\nwill continue on the very warm side for late October, winds will\nlikely be unpleasant by midday Friday, with gusts of 40 to 50 \nMPH possible across the entire area.\n\nAs the west coast trough then lifts out of the southwest late in\nthe weekend, precipitation chances should begin to increase, \nwith rainfall becoming likely across much of the area \nSunday/Monday. Give the very minimal instability, cannot rule \nout a thunderstorm over this time period, but a strong or severe\nthunderstorm appears unlikely. As this system pushes east early\nnext week, cooler air will filter in from the north, dropping \ntemperatures back down closer to climatology (upper 50s to near \n60). These cooler temps, along with much more limited (if any) \nchances for precip will then linger through at least the end of \nnext week as a progressive and generally zonal upper level flow\npattern is realized.",
